                <pb facs="0267" n="259" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg259"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.11/2004: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName> [<date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content-and-sibling-dates"
                            calendar="gregorian" notAfter="1937-08-16T02:30:00-04:00"
                            notBefore="1937-08-15T17:00:00+08:00">undated</date>].<lb/>[Received
                        August 16, 1937—2:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The Commander-in-Chief of the Asiatic Fleet and I have considered it necessary in
                    the extreme emergency and without advance specific authorization from Washington
                    to direct the Dollar Steamship Company’s general manager for the Far East to
                    divert their ships from regular schedules to evacuate American women and
                    children from Shanghai to Manila. I respectfully request that any necessary
                    action be obtained from Congress to support the emergency action we have taken.
                    Details will be communicated later.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
